{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 53, :comments ["<b>[ויוצא</b> — like ויוציא (and he brought out).]" "<b>And the servant brought out vessels of silver.</b> These he gave for the purpose of kiddushin, and the first ones for the purpose of a gift; the matchmaking gifts precede and then the kiddushin follows. According to the plain sense, he gave the precious items to her, for he knew that they would return with her, and the lighter items he gave to her brother and her mother, but Betuel did not need appeasement — he was happy to give his daughter to Yitzhak, his cousin's son. So too, 'her brother and her mother said,' but Betuel had no desire to hold back, for he desired the match because he was his kinsman. According to the plain sense, because of the gifts they received, they spoke first; or perhaps Lavan was greater than his father in wisdom. And it is found in the Aggadah: Betuel sought to feed Eliezer a deadly poison so that he would inherit the money, and the angel Gavriel came and switched the platter and placed it before Betuel; therefore Scripture does not mention him here. Another interpretation: the people of his generation called his name Betuel because all the virgins were first ravished by him, and he ruled over Aram, and the people of his city wanted him to violate even Rivka his daughter before she would go to her marriage, and if not, they would kill him; and he consented to the matter, and Gavriel came and killed him."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "The word אחור in the phrase 'ולא היה חפץ באחור' has been translated literally as 'to hold back'; the word may be corrupt or a variant spelling."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:45.417893511Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}